# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values.
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed.

# --- Public base URL (App\Service\AppUrl) ---
# Used to build canonical links, og:url and absolute og:image URLs.
#
# THE ORDER, and there is only one (see SETUP.md):
#   1. APP_URL below                      deployment configuration
#   2. site_settings.canonical_base_url   what the Setup Wizard writes, for an
#                                         installation whose owner cannot edit
#                                         this file
#   3. https://localhost                  an obvious placeholder meaning
#                                         "nobody has said"
# Step 2 sits BEHIND this variable on purpose: fill APP_URL in and it decides,
# and the CMS then shows the field read-only rather than pretending it is the
# owner's to change. The Host header is never consulted in any of the three.
#
# CONFIGURE THIS BEFORE GOING TO PRODUCTION. Canonical/OG URLs are meant to
# always name the real, indexable production URL, regardless of where the HTML
# happens to be rendered from — so fill in the production domain here (or let
# the Setup Wizard write it) and leave it alone even for local Docker testing.
# Left empty, the chain above ends at https://localhost, which is visibly a
# placeholder rather than quietly somebody else's domain.
#
# This file ships with every copy of this application, so it names no site.
#   APP_URL=https://www.example-site.test
APP_URL=

# --- Environment (App\Service\AppEnvironment) ---
# Which deployment this is. Read by exactly one thing today: /robots.txt.
# A production deployment serves the normal crawler directives; anything set
# to "local", "development", "dev", "staging", "test" or "testing" serves
# "Disallow: /" instead, so a staging or test copy of the site cannot compete
# with the real one in the search index.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS PRODUCTION. Missing, empty, unreadable or misspelt all mean
# production, exactly like the module switches: a configuration mistake can
# never take the live site out of Google. The hostname is deliberately never
# consulted — a request must not be able to talk the application into
# believing it is a test server.
#
# APP_ENV=production

# --- First-party modules (App\Module\ModuleRegistry) ---
# Which optional parts of the application run. One variable per module,
# MODULE_<KEY>_ENABLED, and a module that nobody configures gets its OWN
# default. For the Shop and Personalisatie that default is ENABLED: leave those
# out (or leave them empty) and they run. Only an explicit off value switches
# one off — "false", "0", "off", "no" or "disabled"; anything else, including
# a typo, means on, so a mistake here can never silently take a shop offline.
#
# A SITE THAT SELLS NOTHING SHOULD SAY SO: set MODULE_SHOP_ENABLED=false
# below. The Shop's default is ENABLED because it predates the module system
# and every installation that exists today runs on that default — flipping it
# would take a live storefront off the air on the next deploy. So a CMS-only
# site switches it off explicitly, in one line, right here.
#
# The BLOG is the exception and defaults to OFF: not every site writes
# articles, and an empty blog on a live /blog URL is worse than no blog. Set
# MODULE_BLOG_ENABLED=true below (or tick it in the CMS) to switch it on.
#
# The PORTFOLIO defaults to OFF on a new installation as well: not every site
# shows earlier work. An installation that already ran it — it used to be part
# of the core — keeps it, because a migration stored that choice in the CMS.
# Set MODULE_PORTFOLIO_ENABLED below to decide it here instead.
#
# A variable set here WINS over the choice made in the CMS (the Setup Wizard
# stores one in `module_settings` for installations that cannot edit this
# file — see MODULES.md and SETUP.md). Leaving these commented out is what
# lets the CMS decide; filling one in pins it on the server.
#
# Disabling a module does not uninstall anything. Its code stays in place and
# its database tables are never touched (products, orders, personalization and
# the rest are left exactly as they are); the module simply contributes no
# behaviour: no admin sections, no permissions, no routes, no content blocks,
# no sitemap entries and no frontend assets. Switching it back on restores
# everything, including CMS users' stored permissions. See MODULES.md.
#
#   shop             products, collections, cart, checkout, orders, payments,
#                    invoices and shipping.
#   personalization  DEPENDS ON shop. Asking for it while the shop is off
#                    gets it switched off too, with one line in the error log
#                    — it is never run half-on.
#   blog             blog posts with categories, tags, a public /blog page and
#                    an RSS feed. DEFAULTS TO OFF; see BLOG.md.
#   portfolio        work items with categories and optional project pages,
#                    shown through a gallery block. DEFAULTS TO OFF on a new
#                    installation; see MODULES.md.
#
# MODULE_SHOP_ENABLED=true
# MODULE_PERSONALIZATION_ENABLED=true
# MODULE_BLOG_ENABLED=false
# MODULE_PORTFOLIO_ENABLED=false

# --- Local Docker installation (docker-compose.yml) ---
# Every clone of this repository is an installation of its own: its own
# containers, its own MySQL server, its own uploads. Docker Compose keeps them
# apart by the project name, which is the name of the directory the clone sits
# in ("mygdala", "mygdala-test", "klant-a"), so there is nothing to set for
# that.
#
# What installations running side by side DO have to settle between them are
# the ports on this machine, because each can be taken only once. These three
# are all of them: what a person opens in a browser. Give every installation
# its own numbers, for example 8000/8080/8025 for one and 8100/8180/8125 for
# the next. MySQL, Mailpit's SMTP port and the test web servers publish no
# fixed port, so they cannot collide. See README.md.
#
# Without Docker (Vimexx) nothing reads these three.
APP_PORT=8000
ADMINER_PORT=8080
MAILPIT_WEB_PORT=8025
#
# Only for two clones in directories with the SAME name, say C:\sites\mygdala
# and D:\work\mygdala: Compose would take them for one project and let them
# share containers and a database. Lowercase letters, digits, "-" and "_".
#   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=mygdala-work

# --- Local development database (Docker Compose) ---
# Every Docker installation has its own MySQL server, so these names may stay
# the same in every clone. The mysql container creates the user and the
# database from these values the first time it starts on an empty volume;
# after that they have to keep matching that volume.
#
# DB_HOST and DB_PORT are for a server without Docker. Inside Docker the PHP
# containers always use mysql:3306 (docker-compose.yml pins both), and MySQL
# publishes no port on this machine: use Adminer to look inside.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=3306
DB_DATABASE=mygdala
DB_USERNAME=mygdala_app
DB_PASSWORD=change_me_local_dev_password
DB_ROOT_PASSWORD=change_me_root_password

# --- Test database (PHPUnit) ---
# The suite never runs against DB_DATABASE. tests/bootstrap.php forces the
# connection onto this database instead and refuses to start if the two are
# the same. Both values below are optional: the defaults are
# "<DB_DATABASE>_test" and the php_test container's own hostname.
# Build/refresh the database with:
#   docker compose exec php php scripts/test-db.php
# See TESTING.md.
# TEST_DB_DATABASE=mygdala_tests
#
# Where the HTTP-level tests send their requests. This must be a web server
# wired to TEST_DB_DATABASE — the php_test container from docker-compose.yml
# (`docker compose --profile test up -d`), NOT the development site on
# http://localhost, or the HTTP tier would write to development content.
# TEST_BASE_URL=http://php_test
#
# And where the CMS-only tests send theirs: the php_cms container, the same
# code and the same test database with MODULE_SHOP_ENABLED=false (and the Blog
# and the Portfolio switched off as well). It is what
# proves a shop-less deployment really boots. Those tests skip themselves when
# it is not running. From this machine, `docker compose port php_cms 80` names
# the port Docker gave it.
# TEST_CMS_BASE_URL=http://php_cms

# --- Mollie (test key for local development) ---
MOLLIE_API_KEY=test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile (checkout bot protection, App\Service\TurnstileVerifier) ---
# The secret key is used server-side ONLY (Cloudflare Siteverify) — it is
# never sent to the browser. The site key is public and rendered into
# checkout.php on purpose.
#
# Get real keys: Cloudflare dashboard -> Turnstile -> Add site -> widget mode
# "Managed" -> add your production domain (and any staging domain you use).
# This gives you one site key + one secret key pair; put the real pair only
# in the server's own .env on Vimexx, never here or in git.
#
# Local development/testing: use Cloudflare's own official, publicly
# documented test keys below (always pass, no real challenge is shown) —
# never leave these two variables empty, since App\Service\TurnstileVerifier
# deliberately fails checkout closed (not open) whenever either key is
# unset, rather than silently skipping verification.
#   TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
# (Cloudflare also publishes an "always blocks" pair, 2x00000000000000000000AB /
# 2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA, useful for testing the failure path.)
#
# Production (Vimexx): the real site key/secret key pair from the Cloudflare
# dashboard for this domain — required, Turnstile is always enforced there.
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Automatic content translation (App\Service\Translation, MULTILINGUAL.md) ---
#
# ENTIRELY OPTIONAL. Leave all three empty and this CMS works exactly as it
# does with them: every editor opens, both languages can be typed by hand, and
# the only difference is that no "Translate to ..." button is offered. A fresh
# Mygdala installation is expected to boot without translation credentials.
#
# The key is read by PHP and used server-side only. It is never rendered into
# HTML or JavaScript, never put in a URL, and never written to a log — see
# App\Service\Translation\DeepLProvider, and
# Tests\Service\MultilingualBoundaryTest, which fails if that changes.
#
# TRANSLATION_PROVIDER   which provider to use. Today the only registered one
#                        is "deepl"; anything else (including empty) means no
#                        automatic translation.
# DEEPL_API_KEY          your key. NEVER COMMIT A REAL ONE.
# DEEPL_API_PLAN         "free" or "pro". DeepL serves the two plans from
#                        DIFFERENT HOSTNAMES and rejects a key sent to the
#                        wrong one. DeepL marks its own free keys with a ":fx"
#                        suffix, and such a key selects the free host whatever
#                        this says — so this only matters for a paid key.
TRANSLATION_PROVIDER=
DEEPL_API_KEY=
DEEPL_API_PLAN=free

# --- Admin login (/admin/) — first Super Admin + break-glass ---
# CMS accounts live in the database (`admin_users`, see MAIN.MD "CMS-gebruikers
# en rechten"); this pair is no longer the everyday login. It has two jobs:
#   1. On a fresh install, db/migrations/*_create_admin_users_tables.php turns it
#      into the FIRST Super Admin, reusing this exact hash — so the account you
#      configure here keeps working unchanged after the migration.
#   2. Afterwards it is the break-glass credential: App\Service\AdminAuth only
#      falls back to it when the database holds no active Super Admin at all
#      (migration not run yet, database unreachable, rows lost). On a successful
#      break-glass login the Super Admin row is (re)created automatically. As
#      long as a real Super Admin account exists, this pair does nothing.
# Keep it configured and keep it secret — it is the recovery path.
# Never store the plain password anywhere — only its bcrypt hash. Generate one with:
#   docker compose exec php php scripts/generate_admin_hash.php "your-password"
# IMPORTANT (Docker Compose only): double every literal $ as $$ below, otherwise
# Compose's own .env parsing mistakes "$abc" for a variable reference and silently
# blanks it out. On production (Vimexx, no Docker) paste the hash with single $ signs
# instead, since plain PHP dotenv reads this file directly without that substitution.
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=change_me_generate_with_scripts/generate_admin_hash.php

# --- Outgoing mail (order confirmation emails) ---
# Local dev (Docker): docker-compose.yml forces MAIL_HOST=mailpit and
# MAIL_PORT=1025 for the PHP containers regardless of what's set below, so
# local development ALWAYS uses Mailpit (http://localhost:<MAILPIT_WEB_PORT>)
# and can never accidentally send real mail — MAIL_HOST and MAIL_PORT below
# only matter where there is no Docker.
# No auth/encryption needed for Mailpit; leave MAIL_USERNAME/MAIL_PASSWORD empty.
#
# Production (shared hosting, no Docker): this file is read directly, so fill
# in the REAL SMTP mailbox details from your hosting control panel (E-mail ->
# the mailbox you want to send from). Typical values, to verify in the panel
# for your own domain:
#   MAIL_HOST=mail.your-domain.example       (or the panel's own SMTP host)
#   MAIL_PORT=587
#   MAIL_ENCRYPTION=tls                      (STARTTLS on port 587; use ssl+465 if the panel says so)
#   MAIL_USERNAME=the full mailbox address you send from
#   MAIL_PASSWORD=that mailbox's password
# Never commit real production values here — only in the server's own .env.
MAIL_HOST=127.0.0.1
MAIL_PORT=1025
MAIL_USERNAME=
MAIL_PASSWORD=
# none | tls | ssl
MAIL_ENCRYPTION=none
# WHO TRANSACTIONAL MAIL COMES FROM. Configure this pair before switching
# transactional email on; until then nothing is sent as anybody.
#
# Both are optional here and both fall back to what the CMS already knows
# (App\Mail\EmailIdentity): the address falls back to Instellingen ->
# Contact-e-mailadres and the name to the site's own name. Fill them in when
# the mail provider only lets you send as one specific mailbox, which is the
# usual case on shared hosting. If neither this pair nor those settings name
# a sender, App\Service\Mailer refuses to send and says so in the log rather
# than sending as nobody.
MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=
MAIL_FROM_NAME=
# Where the shop's own "new paid order" notification is sent. Required as
# soon as the Shop takes orders — if this is empty, OrderConfirmationService
# will NOT send either email (and will not mark the order as confirmed)
# rather than sending only half.
SHOP_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL=

# --- Contact-request attachment storage (App\Service\ContactAttachmentStorage) ---
# Optional. Leave empty to use the default: a "storage/contact-attachments"
# folder one directory ABOVE the project root — genuinely outside whatever
# the web server's document root is, not just .htaccess-blocked.
#   - Local Docker: the default already resolves to /var/www/storage/..., a
#     dedicated named volume outside /var/www/html (docker-compose.yml) — no
#     need to set this locally.
#   - Vimexx: if "upload the folder via SSH/FTP" (see README) really does put
#     this project directly in the account's document root, the same default
#     lands one level above it, which is normally readable/writable by the
#     same hosting-account user. This has NOT been SSH-verified yet (first
#     real deploy is still an open item in MAIN.MD) — if it turns out the
#     account layout doesn't allow that, set this to any other absolute path
#     outside the document root that the account can read/write, e.g.:
#   CONTACT_ATTACHMENTS_PATH=/home/your-vimexx-user/storage
CONTACT_ATTACHMENTS_PATH=

# --- Personalization uploads (customer engraving images) ---
# Where a customer's uploaded personalization image is stored. Same model,
# same reasoning and the same default location as CONTACT_ATTACHMENTS_PATH
# above: these are private ORDER data (the owner's production source files),
# so they live genuinely outside the web root and are only ever served
# through an authenticated CMS endpoint. Leave empty to use the default
# (<one directory above the project root>/storage/personalization-uploads).
#   PERSONALIZATION_UPLOADS_PATH=/home/your-vimexx-user/storage
PERSONALIZATION_UPLOADS_PATH=

# --- One-off REAL SMTP test (scripts/send_test_email.php only) ---
# Completely separate from MAIL_* above — the app itself (order emails)
# never reads these, and docker-compose.yml's forced MAIL_HOST=mailpit
# override has no effect on them either. Fill these in only when you want
# to send ONE real test email through the real Vimexx mailbox; leave blank
# otherwise. Never commit real values — this file only holds placeholders.
# Vimexx values for mail.zxcs.nl:
#   TEST_SMTP_HOST=mail.zxcs.nl
#   TEST_SMTP_PORT=465
#   TEST_SMTP_ENCRYPTION=ssl
#   TEST_SMTP_USERNAME=your-full-vimexx-mailbox@yourdomain.nl
#   TEST_SMTP_PASSWORD=that mailbox's real password
#   TEST_SMTP_TO=an inbox you can check (can be the same address, or another one)
TEST_SMTP_HOST=
TEST_SMTP_PORT=
TEST_SMTP_ENCRYPTION=
TEST_SMTP_USERNAME=
TEST_SMTP_PASSWORD=
TEST_SMTP_TO=
